[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Locust: Python-Based Load Testing and Distributed Swarm"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Locust"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Locust is an easy-to-use, scriptable, and scalable performance testing tool. Unlike traditional tools that rely on rigid XML configurations or complex GUIs, Locust allows you to define your user behavior using standard Python code. This makes it highly extensible and ideal for complex testing scenarios."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"User Class: Represents a virtual user that spawns and executes tasks defined within the script."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tasks: Python functions decorated with @task that simulate specific actions (e.g., login, fetch data, submit form)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Distributed Swarm: The ability to scale tests across multiple machine nodes seamlessly to generate massive loads."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing a Basic Locustfile"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a file named locustfile.py. This script defines the user behaviors, weight distributions, and the HTTP requests sent to the target application."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"from locust import HttpUser, task, between

class WebsiteUser(HttpUser):
    wait_time = between(1, 5)

    @task(3)
    def view_index(self):
        self.client.get("/")

    @task(1)
    def view_item(self):
        self.client.get("/item?id=1")"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Running a Standalone Load Test"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To launch Locust with its web interface, run the following command in your terminal:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"locust -f locustfile.py"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Navigate to http://localhost:8089 in your browser to configure the number of total users and the spawn rate."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Scaling with Distributed Swarm Mode"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a single machine cannot generate enough traffic, Locust can run in distributed mode across multiple processes or servers. One machine acts as the master, while others act as workers."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Starting the Master Node"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"locust -f locustfile.py --master"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Starting Worker Nodes"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"locust -f locustfile.py --worker --master-host=127.0.0.1"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Locust provides a powerful, code-centric approach to load testing. By leveraging Python and a distributed master-worker architecture, developers and QA engineers can accurately simulate high-concurrency environments to test application limits."}]
